The Passage Tithe was a payment-in-kind road taxation regime used when coin systems failed.
Convoys paid in practical goods such as grain, salt, lamp oil, and iron nails to keep corridors operating.
The model spread from northern crisis logistics into western road governance, including the Roc Road.
Its legacy is the legal principle that infrastructure continuity can outweigh currency purity during collapse periods.
